<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wu Qi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 440–381 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wu Qi was a general and reformer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a>, born in the State of Wey. He commanded the armies of Wei under Marquis Wen, winning the lands west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>, and later served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-dao-of-chu">King Dao of Chu</a> as chief minister, cutting the privileges of the nobility. After the king's death in 381 BCE nobles killed him beside the royal corpse. The military treatise Wuzi bears his name.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wu-qi.jpg" alt="Wu Qi" width="320" height="422" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wu_Qi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
